# Machine-translated by PLaMo Translate.
#. type: Content of: <html><body><div><hr><p>
#: build/html/eintr/Arguments.html build/html/mono/eintr.html
msgid ""
"It is curious to track the path by which the word &ldquo;argument&rdquo; "
"came to have two different meanings, one in mathematics and the other in "
"everyday English.  According to the <cite class=\"cite\">Oxford English "
"Dictionary</cite>, the word derives from the Latin for &lsquo;<samp "
"class=\"samp\">to make clear, prove</samp>&rsquo;; thus it came to mean, by "
"one thread of derivation, &ldquo;the evidence offered as proof&rdquo;, which "
"is to say, &ldquo;the information offered&rdquo;, which led to its meaning "
"in Lisp.  But in the other thread of derivation, it came to mean &ldquo;to "
"assert in a manner against which others may make counter assertions&rdquo;, "
"which led to the meaning of the word as a disputation.  (Note here that the "
"English word has two different definitions attached to it at the same time.  "
"By contrast, in Emacs Lisp, a symbol cannot have two different function "
"definitions at the same time.)"
msgstr ""
"「argument」という単語が、数学と日常英語という2つの異なる意味を持つに至った経"
"緯を辿るのは興味深い。<cite class=\"cite\">オックスフォード英語辞典</cite>に"
"よれば、この単語はラテン語の「<samp class=\"samp\">to make clear, prove</"
"samp>」に由来しており、したがって一つの派生経路では「証明として提示される証"
"拠」、すなわち「提示される情報」を意味するようになり、それがLispにおける意味"
"へとつながった。しかし、もう一つの派生経路では、「他者が反論しうる形で主張す"
"ること」を意味するようになり、それが論争という意味へとつながった。（ここで注"
"目すべきは、英語の単語に同時に2つの異なる定義が付随している点である。対照的"
"に、Emacs Lispでは、一つのシンボルが同時に2つの異なる関数定義を持つことはでき"
"ない。）"

