# Machine-translated by PLaMo Translate.
#. type: Content of: <html><body><div><br><br><br><br><br><hr><div><hr><div><hr><div>
#: build/html/eintr/Lisp-Atoms.html build/html/mono/eintr.html
msgid "<a class=\"index-entry-id\" id=\"index-Lisp-Atoms\"></a>"
msgstr "<a class=\"index-entry-id\" id=\"index-Lisp-Atoms\"></a>"

# Machine-translated by PLaMo Translate.
#. type: Content of: <html><body><div><hr><p>
#: build/html/eintr/Lisp-Atoms.html
msgid ""
"In Lisp, what we have been calling words are called <em class=\"dfn\">atoms</"
"em>.  This term comes from the historical meaning of the word atom, which "
"means &ldquo;indivisible&rdquo;.  As far as Lisp is concerned, the words we "
"have been using in the lists cannot be divided into any smaller parts and "
"still mean the same thing as part of a program; likewise with numbers and "
"single character symbols like &lsquo;<samp class=\"samp\">+</samp>&rsquo;.  "
"On the other hand, unlike an ancient atom, a list can be split into parts.  "
"(See <a class=\"xref\" href=\"car-cdr-_0026-cons.html\"><code "
"class=\"code\">car</code> <code class=\"code\">cdr</code> &amp; <code "
"class=\"code\">cons</code> Fundamental Functions</a>.)"
msgstr ""
"Lispでは、これまで「単語」と呼んできたものを<em class=\"dfn\">アトム</em>と呼"
"びます。この用語は、「分割不能」を意味するatomという単語の歴史的な意味に由来"
"しています。Lispにおいては、リスト内で使用してきた単語は、プログラムの一部と"
"して同じ意味を保ちつつ、これ以上小さな部分に分割することはできません。数値"
"や、「<samp class=\"samp\">+</samp>」のような単一文字の記号についても同様で"
"す。一方で、古代の原子とは異なり、リストは部分に分割することができます。（<a "
"class=\"xref\" href=\"car-cdr-_0026-cons.html\"><code class=\"code\">car</"
"code> <code class=\"code\">cdr</code> &amp; <code class=\"code\">cons</code> "
"基本関数</a>を参照してください。）"

# Machine-translated by PLaMo Translate.
#. type: Content of: <html><body><div><br><br><br><br><br><hr><div><hr><div><hr><div><p>
#: build/html/eintr/Lisp-Atoms.html build/html/mono/eintr.html
msgid ""
"In a list, atoms are separated from each other by whitespace.  They can be "
"right next to a parenthesis."
msgstr ""
"リストでは、原子同士は空白で区切られます。また、括弧のすぐ隣に配置することも"
"可能です。"

# Machine-translated by PLaMo Translate.
#. type: Content of: <html><body><div><br><br><br><br><br><hr><div><hr><div><hr><div>
#: build/html/eintr/Lisp-Atoms.html build/html/mono/eintr.html
msgid "<a class=\"index-entry-id\" id=\"index-empty-list-defined\"></a>"
msgstr "<a class=\"index-entry-id\" id=\"index-empty-list-defined\"></a>"

# Machine-translated by PLaMo Translate.
#. type: Content of: <html><body><div><br><br><br><br><br><hr><div><hr><div><hr><div><p>
#: build/html/eintr/Lisp-Atoms.html build/html/mono/eintr.html
msgid ""
"Technically speaking, a list in Lisp consists of parentheses surrounding "
"atoms separated by whitespace or surrounding other lists or surrounding both "
"atoms and other lists.  A list can have just one atom in it or have nothing "
"in it at all.  A list with nothing in it looks like this: <code "
"class=\"code\">()</code>, and is called the <em class=\"dfn\">empty list</"
"em>.  Unlike anything else, an empty list is considered both an atom and a "
"list at the same time."
msgstr ""
"厳密に言えば、Lispにおけるリストとは、空白で区切られたアトム、あるいは他のリ"
"スト、あるいはその両方を囲む括弧で構成される。リストにはアトムが1つだけ含まれ"
"ている場合もあれば、全く含まれていない場合もある。何も含まれていないリストは "
"<code class=\"code\">()</code> のように表記され、<em class=\"dfn\">空リスト</"
"em>と呼ばれる。他のあらゆるものとは異なり、空リストはアトムであると同時にリス"
"トであるとみなされる。"

# Machine-translated by PLaMo Translate.
#. type: Content of: <html><body><div><br><br><br><br><br><hr><div><hr><div><hr><div>
#: build/html/eintr/Lisp-Atoms.html build/html/mono/eintr.html
msgid ""
"<a class=\"index-entry-id\" id=\"index-Symbolic-expressions_002c-"
"introduced\"></a> <a class=\"index-entry-id\" id=\"index-expression-"
"defined\"></a> <a class=\"index-entry-id\" id=\"index-form-defined\"></a>"
msgstr ""
"<a class=\"index-entry-id\" id=\"index-Symbolic-expressions_002c-"
"introduced\"></a> <a class=\"index-entry-id\" id=\"index-expression-"
"defined\"></a> <a class=\"index-entry-id\" id=\"index-form-defined\"></a>"

# Machine-translated by PLaMo Translate.
#. type: Content of: <html><body><div><br><br><br><br><br><hr><div><hr><div><hr><div><p>
#: build/html/eintr/Lisp-Atoms.html build/html/mono/eintr.html
msgid ""
"The printed representation of both atoms and lists are called <em "
"class=\"dfn\">symbolic expressions</em> or, more concisely, <em "
"class=\"dfn\">s-expressions</em>.  The word <em class=\"dfn\">expression</"
"em> by itself can refer to either the printed representation, or to the atom "
"or list as it is held internally in the computer.  Often, people use the "
"term <em class=\"dfn\">expression</em> indiscriminately.  (Also, in many "
"texts, the word <em class=\"dfn\">form</em> is used as a synonym for "
"expression.)"
msgstr ""
"原子とリストの両方の表記形式は、<em class=\"dfn\">記号式</em>、あるいはより簡"
"潔に<em class=\"dfn\">s式</em>と呼ばれる。<em class=\"dfn\">式</em>という言葉"
"単体では、表記形式、あるいはコンピュータ内部で保持されている原子やリストのい"
"ずれかを指すことがある。多くの場合、人々は<em class=\"dfn\">式</em>という用語"
"を区別なく用いている。（また、多くの文献では、<em class=\"dfn\">形式</em>とい"
"う言葉が式の同義語として用いられている。）"

# Machine-translated by PLaMo Translate.
#. type: Content of: <html><body><div><br><br><br><br><br><hr><div><hr><div><hr><div><p>
#: build/html/eintr/Lisp-Atoms.html build/html/mono/eintr.html
msgid ""
"Incidentally, the atoms that make up our universe were named such when they "
"were thought to be indivisible; but it has been found that physical atoms "
"are not indivisible.  Parts can split off an atom or it can fission into two "
"parts of roughly equal size.  Physical atoms were named prematurely, before "
"their truer nature was found.  In Lisp, certain kinds of atom, such as an "
"array, can be separated into parts; but the mechanism for doing this is "
"different from the mechanism for splitting a list.  As far as list "
"operations are concerned, the atoms of a list are unsplittable."
msgstr ""
"ちなみに、我々の宇宙を構成する原子は、不可分であると考えられていた時代にそう"
"名付けられました。しかし、物理的な原子は不可分ではないことが判明しています。"
"原子から一部が分離することもあれば、ほぼ等しい大きさの2つの部分に核分裂するこ"
"ともあります。物理的な原子は、その真の性質が解明される前に、時期尚早に命名さ"
"れたのです。Lispでは、配列などの特定の種類の原子を部分に分割できますが、その"
"仕組みはリストを分割する仕組みとは異なります。リスト操作に関しては、リストの"
"原子は分割不可能です。"

# Machine-translated by PLaMo Translate.
#. type: Content of: <html><body><div><br><br><br><br><br><hr><div><hr><div><hr><div><p>
#: build/html/eintr/Lisp-Atoms.html build/html/mono/eintr.html
msgid ""
"As in English, the meanings of the component letters of a Lisp atom are "
"different from the meaning the letters make as a word.  For example, the "
"word for the South American sloth, the &lsquo;<samp class=\"samp\">ai</"
"samp>&rsquo;, is completely different from the two words, &lsquo;<samp "
"class=\"samp\">a</samp>&rsquo;, and &lsquo;<samp class=\"samp\">i</"
"samp>&rsquo;."
msgstr ""
"英語と同様に、Lispアトムの構成文字が持つ意味は、それらの文字が単語として持つ"
"意味とは異なります。例えば、南米のナマケモノを指す単語 &lsquo;<samp "
"class=\"samp\">ai</samp>&rsquo; は、&lsquo;<samp class=\"samp\">a</"
"samp>&rsquo; と &lsquo;<samp class=\"samp\">i</samp>&rsquo; という2つの単語と"
"は全く異なります。"

# Machine-translated by PLaMo Translate.
#. type: Content of: <html><body><div><br><br><br><br><br><hr><div><hr><div><hr><div><p>
#: build/html/eintr/Lisp-Atoms.html build/html/mono/eintr.html
msgid ""
"There are many kinds of atom in nature but only a few in Lisp: for example, "
"<em class=\"dfn\">numbers</em>, such as 37, 511, or 1729, and <em "
"class=\"dfn\">symbols</em>, such as &lsquo;<samp class=\"samp\">+</"
"samp>&rsquo;, &lsquo;<samp class=\"samp\">foo</samp>&rsquo;, or &lsquo;<samp "
"class=\"samp\">forward-line</samp>&rsquo;.  The words we have listed in the "
"examples above are all symbols.  In everyday Lisp conversation, the word "
"&ldquo;atom&rdquo; is not often used, because programmers usually try to be "
"more specific about what kind of atom they are dealing with.  Lisp "
"programming is mostly about symbols (and sometimes numbers)  within lists.  "
"(Incidentally, the preceding three word parenthetical remark is a proper "
"list in Lisp, since it consists of atoms, which in this case are symbols, "
"separated by whitespace and enclosed by parentheses, without any non-Lisp "
"punctuation.)"
msgstr ""
"自然界には多くの種類の原子が存在しますが、Lispにあるのはごくわずかです。例え"
"ば、37、511、1729といった<em class=\"dfn\">数値</em>や、&lsquo;<samp "
"class=\"samp\">+</samp>&rsquo;、&lsquo;<samp class=\"samp\">foo</"
"samp>&rsquo;、&lsquo;<samp class=\"samp\">forward-line</samp>&rsquo;といった"
"<em class=\"dfn\">シンボル</em>などが挙げられます。上記の例に挙げた単語は、す"
"べてシンボルです。日常的なLispの会話では、「原子」という言葉はあまり使われま"
"せん。というのも、プログラマーは通常、自分がどのような種類の原子を扱っている"
"のかについて、より具体的に述べようとするからです。Lispプログラミングの大部分"
"は、リスト内のシンボル（および時には数値）を扱うものです。（ちなみに、前述の3"
"つの単語からなる括弧内の記述は、Lispにおける正当なリストです。なぜなら、これ"
"は空白で区切られ、括弧で囲まれた原子（この場合はシンボル）で構成されており、"
"Lisp以外の句読点が一切使われていないからです。）"

# Machine-translated by PLaMo Translate.
#. type: Content of: <html><body><div><br><br><br><br><br><hr><div><hr><div><hr><div><p>
#: build/html/eintr/Lisp-Atoms.html build/html/mono/eintr.html
msgid ""
"Text between double quotation marks&mdash;even sentences or "
"paragraphs&mdash;is also an atom.  Here is an example: <a class=\"index-"
"entry-id\" id=\"index-Text-between-double-quotation-marks\"></a>"
msgstr ""
"ダブルクォーテーションで囲まれたテキスト&mdash;文や段落であっても&mdash;もア"
"トムです。以下に例を示します：<a class=\"index-entry-id\" id=\"index-Text-"
"between-double-quotation-marks\"></a>"

